# Trivy vulnerability waivers for the orchestration content image.
#
# kubectl (usr/local/bin/kubectl, orchestration/Dockerfile) is flagged for CVEs in the
# Go code it is compiled against, not in kubectl's own code. The first two are in the
# Go stdlib: no kubectl release built on a patched Go (1.26.9 / 1.27.2) exists yet —
# v1.36.5 (the EKS-pinned minor) and v1.37.1 (latest) both ship go1.26.8 — so there is
# nothing to bump to. Both are denial-of-service on server-side surfaces the worker's
# kubectl never exposes: it is a client talking only to the AWS-managed EKS API, never an
# HTTP or TLS server.
#
# CVE-2026-78669 (golang.org/x/net http2 and the stdlib's bundled copy) differs: a
# malicious HTTP/2 peer can burn CPU in a client as well as a server, so the worker's
# kubectl is in reach. Its one peer is the AWS-managed EKS API endpoint, reached over TLS
# verified against the cluster CA, so exploiting it needs a compromised control plane or
# a broken TLS chain, and the impact is CPU exhaustion of the worker pod. Its fixes
# (x/net 0.60.0, Go 1.26.9 / 1.27.2) are in no kubectl release yet either.
#
# Drop each line once upstream kubectl rebuilds on the patched Go. The exp date forces
# the scan to fail again and this file to be revisited rather than rot silently.
CVE-2026-78667 exp:2026-11-08
CVE-2026-97031 exp:2026-11-08
CVE-2026-78669 exp:2026-11-08
